  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLatest surface analysis places a west to east cold front across\nsouthern Quebec. Latest model guidance suggest this front will\nslowly sink south today through tonight and will provide a focus\nfor clouds and showers. Skies will be mostly cloudy for much of\nthe area, except for southern NH, while showers chances will be\nmostly confined to the foothills and points northward today.\nAcross southern NH where there will more sun, temperatures will\npush into the low 70s with temperatures decreasing to the north\nand east.\n\nShower chances will expand southward early tonight before\nretreating northward going into Wednesday morning. The latest\nNBM generally keeps QPF below a tenth of an inch along and north\nof the mountains while 00Z CAMs suggest QPF could approach 0.25\nto 0.5 inches along and north of the mountains. \n\nThe front will lift north of the area Wednesday leading to a\nmostly dry day. Skies will turn partly to mostly sunny along\nwith temperatures at 850 mb climbing to +12C to +14C. This will\npush highs well into the 70s Wednesday with records in jeopardy\nat CON (75 in 1987)and PWM (74 in 1987). Above normal\ntemperatures likely continue into Thursday ahead of a slow\nmoving cold front discussed below.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA deep trough that brought flooding to parts of the Southwest \nis currently progressing into the PLains with models showing the\ntrough closing off into an upper low. This upper low will weaken\nas it lifts northeast through the Great Lakes Thursday with an \nattendant cold front approaching from the west. All the while, \nanother trough moving into California today will quickly cross \nthe CONUS and short waves out of Canada may help spawn waves of \nlow pressure along the cold front Friday into Saturday. While \nconfidence is increasing that much of the area will see \nmeasurable rainfall late Thursday into Saturday, confidence is \nlow on amounts with multiple moving parts needing to come \ntogether for an organized area of low pressure. Ensemble \nprobabilities are around 60-70 percent for 0.5 inches during the\nThursday to Saturday timeframe across much of the area. Ensembles\ndo suggest there will be ample moisture available with PWATs \nrunning greater than 200 percent of normal Thursday through \nSaturday. While PoPs drop off Sunday, the pattern looks to \nremain unsettled into early next week with additional troughs \nswinging across the Northeast.",
